1
00:00:04,405 --> 00:00:06,987
CLOCK CHIMES

2
00:00:08,639 --> 00:00:12,674
Whoever those aliens,
they've been here for a while.

3
00:00:12,714 --> 00:00:15,154
Harriet Jones, MP for Flydale North.

4
00:00:15,194 --> 00:00:18,100
I thought I was Prime Minister now.

5
00:00:24,861 --> 00:00:28,657
Aaaaagh!

6
00:00:28,697 --> 00:00:32,935
Thank you all for wearing
your ID cards.

7
00:00:32,975 --> 00:00:35,881
They'll help to identify the bodies.

8
00:00:37,606 --> 00:00:40,348
THE DOCTOR SCREAMS

9
00:00:53,032 --> 00:00:56,104
Deadly to humans, maybe. . .

10
00:00:59,508 --> 00:01:02,454
ALIEN SCREAMS

11
00:01:42,461 --> 00:01:44,918
ALIEN SCREAMS

12
00:01:49,932 --> 00:01:53,584
ALIEN SCREECHES

13
00:01:59,120 --> 00:02:02,112
Ah!

14
00:02:10,788 --> 00:02:13,245
Jackie!

15
00:02:15,266 --> 00:02:18,667
ALIEN ROARS

16
00:02:27,173 --> 00:02:29,247
ALIEN SCREECHES

17
00:02:29,287 --> 00:02:33,468
You want aliens, you've got them.
They're inside Downing Street.

18
00:02:45,550 --> 00:02:49,975
Reinstate my disguise me!
Hurry up, hurry!

19
00:02:52,064 --> 00:02:57,468
Wait! They're still in there - the
Emergency Protocols, we need them.

20
00:03:00,975 --> 00:03:04,661
Aaaagh!

21
00:03:22,592 --> 00:03:24,988
Where've you been?

22
00:03:25,028 --> 00:03:28,545
I called for help,
I sounded the alarm. . .

23
00:03:28,585 --> 00:03:34,814
there was this. . .lightning,
this kind of, um, electricity,
and they all collapsed.

24
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:40,560
I think they're all dead.
That's what I'm saying.

25
00:03:40,096 --> 00:03:42,971
He did it! That man there!

26
00:03:43,011 --> 00:03:45,269
The Prime Minister is an alien
in disguise.

27
00:03:48,722 --> 00:03:51,242
That's never gonna work, is it? No.

28
00:03:51,282 --> 00:03:52,925
Fair enough.

29
00:03:55,037 --> 00:03:57,414
Down there!

30
00:04:03,667 --> 00:04:09,505
Under the jurisdiction of the
Emergency Protocols, I authorise
you to execute this man!

31
00:04:09,545 --> 00:04:12,902
Well, now, yes, you see, er,
the thing is. . .

32
00:04:12,942 --> 00:04:17,814
If I was gonna execute someone by
backing him up against the wall,

33
00:04:17,854 --> 00:04:21,625
little word of advice -
don't stand him against the lift.

34
00:04:26,122 --> 00:04:28,600
ALIEN GROWLS

35
00:04:28,640 --> 00:04:29,919
LIFT BELL PINGS

36
00:04:31,280 --> 00:04:32,793
ALIEN HISSES
Hello!

37
00:04:43,309 --> 00:04:44,458
Hide!

38
00:04:54,497 --> 00:04:59,447
I repeat, the upper
floors are under quarantine.

39
00:04:59,487 --> 00:05:04,202
You will disregard all
previous instructions, you will
take your orders directly from me.

40
00:05:04,242 --> 00:05:05,842
LIFT BELL PINGS

41
00:05:05,882 --> 00:05:10,276
Mr Green, sir, sorry,
but you've got to come with me.

42
00:05:10,316 --> 00:05:12,236
We should evacuate the building.

43
00:05:12,276 --> 00:05:16,430
Sergeant, have you, um,
read the Emergency Protocols?

44
00:05:16,470 --> 00:05:18,631
No, sir. Then don't question me.

45
00:05:18,671 --> 00:05:22,670
Seal off Number Ten,
secure the ground floor,

46
00:05:22,107 --> 00:05:28,540
and if the Doctor makes it
downstairs, shoot on sight!

47
00:05:31,491 --> 00:05:33,150
You heard him, move out!

48
00:05:37,846 --> 00:05:41,524
Let the sport begin.
JOSEPH FARTS

49
00:05:41,564 --> 00:05:44,760
I'm getting
poisoned by the gas exchange.

50
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:48,436
I need to be naked. Rejoice in it.

51
00:05:48,476 --> 00:05:52,420
Your body is magnificent.

52
00:06:10,413 --> 00:06:13,487
Oh, such fun.

53
00:06:13,527 --> 00:06:19,676
Little human children,
where are you?

54
00:06:20,721 --> 00:06:23,508
Sweet little human kins,
come to me.

55
00:06:25,318 --> 00:06:27,595
Let me kiss you better.

56
00:06:27,635 --> 00:06:31,201
Kiss you with my big green lips.

57
00:06:35,546 --> 00:06:37,743
SHOUTING

58
00:06:37,783 --> 00:06:40,485
Go and check it out!

59
00:06:42,938 --> 00:06:44,697
LIFT BELL PINGS

60
00:06:44,737 --> 00:06:48,622
It does us good to hunt,
it purifies the blood.

61
00:06:50,529 --> 00:06:55,569
We'll keep this floor
quarantined as our last hiding
ground before the final phase.

62
00:06:59,201 --> 00:07:01,277
My brothers. Happy hunting?

63
00:07:01,317 --> 00:07:03,477
It's wonderful.

64
00:07:03,517 --> 00:07:08,350
The more you prolong it,
the more they stink.

65
00:07:08,390 --> 00:07:10,468
Sweat and fear!

66
00:07:10,508 --> 00:07:13,305
I can smell an old girl.

67
00:07:13,345 --> 00:07:17,741
Stale perfume and brittle bones.

68
00:07:17,781 --> 00:07:22,896
And a ripe youngster,
all hormones and adrenalin.

69
00:07:22,936 --> 00:07:26,912
Fresh enough to bend
before she snaps.

70
00:07:28,130 --> 00:07:31,941
Ah! No! Take me first, take me!

71
00:07:35,162 --> 00:07:36,277
Out! With me!

72
00:07:40,956 --> 00:07:45,666
Who are you?
Harriet Jones, MP for Flydale North.
Nice to meet you. Likewise.

73
00:07:50,305 --> 00:07:52,230
We need the Cabinet Rooms.

74
00:07:52,063 --> 00:07:54,864
The Emergency Protocols
are in there.

75
00:07:54,904 --> 00:07:57,526
Harriet Jones, I like you.
I think I like you too.

76
00:08:24,468 --> 00:08:28,824
One move and my device'll triplicate
the flammability of this alcohol.

77
00:08:28,864 --> 00:08:30,633
Woof, we all go up. So back off.

78
00:08:33,182 --> 00:08:35,990
Right, then, question time.

79
00:08:35,139 --> 00:08:36,773
Who exactly are the Slitheen?

80
00:08:36,813 --> 00:08:38,934
They're aliens.
Yes, I got that, thanks.

81
00:08:38,974 --> 00:08:41,100
Who are you, if not human?

82
00:08:41,050 --> 00:08:44,247
Who? He's not human. He's not?
Could I have a bit of hush? Sorry.

83
00:08:44,287 --> 00:08:45,804
So, what's the plan?

84
00:08:45,844 --> 00:08:49,760
But he's got a northern accent.
Lots of planets have a north. Hush!

85
00:08:49,800 --> 00:08:51,200
Come on!

86
00:08:51,240 --> 00:08:54,780
You've got a spaceship hidden
in the North Sea.

87
00:08:54,118 --> 00:08:57,355
You've murdered your way to the top,
what for, invasion?

88
00:08:57,395 --> 00:09:00,312
Why would we invade this
godforsaken rock?

89
00:09:00,352 --> 00:09:02,669
Something's brought your race here.

90
00:09:02,709 --> 00:09:07,904
The Slitheen race?
Slitheen is not our species.

91
00:09:07,944 --> 00:09:10,340
Slitheen is our surname.

92
00:09:10,380 --> 00:09:14,893
Jocrassa Fel Fotch Pasameer-Day
Slitheen at your service.

93
00:09:14,933 --> 00:09:17,371
So you're family? A family business.

94
00:09:17,411 --> 00:09:19,729
Then you're out to make a profit.

95
00:09:19,769 --> 00:09:21,617
How can you do that on a rock?

96
00:09:23,286 --> 00:09:29,390
Aaaaaaah, excuse me.
Your device will do what -
"triplicate the flammability"?

97
00:09:29,079 --> 00:09:33,237
That's what I said? You're making
it up. Oh, well. Nice try.

98
00:09:33,277 --> 00:09:35,274
Have a drink. You're gonna need it.

99
00:09:35,314 --> 00:09:37,831
You pass it to the left first.
Sorry.

100
00:09:37,871 --> 00:09:42,508
Thanks. Now we can end this
hunt. . .with a slaughter.

101
00:09:42,548 --> 00:09:45,425
Don't you think we should run?

102
00:09:45,465 --> 00:09:48,220
Fascinating history, Downing Street.

103
00:09:48,062 --> 00:09:50,299
2, 000 years ago, this was marshland.

104
00:09:50,339 --> 00:09:53,577
1730, it was occupied by a
Mr Chicken. He was a nice man.

105
00:09:53,617 --> 00:09:56,530
1796, this was the Cabinet Room.

106
00:09:56,570 --> 00:09:58,446
If the Cabinet's in danger,

107
00:09:58,486 --> 00:10:02,917
these are about the safest walls
in Britain. End of lesson.

108
00:10:07,838 --> 00:10:11,876
Installed in 1991. Three inches
of steel, lining every single wall.

109
00:10:11,916 --> 00:10:14,663
They'll never get in.
And how do we get out?

110
00:10:17,069 --> 00:10:18,747
Ah.

111
00:10:18,787 --> 00:10:20,545
He's safely contained.

112
00:10:20,585 --> 00:10:24,223
Now cut off the communications,
then summon up the family!

113
00:10:24,263 --> 00:10:29,470
It's time we finished with
this insane planet for good.

114
00:10:31,372 --> 00:10:34,728
SIRENS WAIL

115
00:10:49,994 --> 00:10:55,933
And there's still no word from
inside Downing Street, though we
are getting even more new arrivals.

116
00:10:56,987 --> 00:11:04,382
That's Group Captain Tennant James
of the RAF, though why
he's been summoned, we've no idea.

117
00:11:04,422 --> 00:11:07,257
And that's Ewan McAllister,

118
00:11:07,297 --> 00:11:11,900
Deputy Secretary for
the Scottish Parliament.

119
00:11:11,049 --> 00:11:13,527
And this is most unusual -

120
00:11:13,567 --> 00:11:20,640
I'm told that is Sylvia Dillane,
chairman of the North Sea
Boating Club.

121
00:11:20,680 --> 00:11:24,201
Quite what connects all these people,
we have no idea.

122
00:11:25,915 --> 00:11:27,632
Group Captain.

123
00:11:27,672 --> 00:11:30,553
Delighted you could make it.
We're meeting upstairs.

124
00:11:30,593 --> 00:11:33,149
HE FARTS
That's the spirit, off you go.

125
00:11:33,189 --> 00:11:37,400
Good to see you. Come on through.

126
00:11:39,463 --> 00:11:46,252
Ah, Sergeant. Now that the Doctor's
been neutralised, the upper levels
are out of bounds to everyone.

127
00:11:46,292 --> 00:11:49,448
Then who are they? Need to
know, Sergeant, need to know.

128
00:11:49,488 --> 00:11:51,966
I want you to liaise
with Communications.

129
00:11:52,006 --> 00:11:54,846
The Acting Prime Minister will
be making an address.

130
00:11:54,886 --> 00:11:57,588
He will speak to
the nations of the world!

131
00:12:01,596 --> 00:12:04,713
If you'd just like to go through
and get changed.

132
00:12:07,831 --> 00:12:11,268
Now, if you'd like to head down to
the end of the corridor,

133
00:12:11,308 --> 00:12:12,864
it's first on the left.

134
00:12:12,904 --> 00:12:14,388
Thank you.

135
00:12:19,382 --> 00:12:21,653
Got anything stronger? No chance.

136
00:12:21,693 --> 00:12:24,940
This ain't time for a conga!

137
00:12:24,134 --> 00:12:25,730
We've gotta tell someone!

138
00:12:25,770 --> 00:12:31,128
Who do we trust?
For all we know, they've all got
big bog monsters inside of 'em.

139
00:12:31,168 --> 00:12:33,400
I mean this is what he does, Jacks.

140
00:12:33,044 --> 00:12:37,758
That Doctor bloke, everywhere he
goes, death and destruction, and
he's got Rose in it.

141
00:12:37,798 --> 00:12:40,875
Has he got a great big
green thing inside him, then?

142
00:12:40,915 --> 00:12:43,232
I wouldn't put it past him.

143
00:12:43,272 --> 00:12:46,668
But he's the only person who knows
how to fight these things!

144
00:12:46,708 --> 00:12:49,110
I thought I was gonna die.

145
00:12:55,736 --> 00:12:58,140
Come on, yeah?

146
00:12:58,054 --> 00:13:00,175
If anyone's gonna
cry, it's gonna be me.

147
00:13:00,215 --> 00:13:01,891
Now, you're safe in my flat.

148
00:13:01,931 --> 00:13:05,169
No-one's gonna look for you here,
since you hate me so much!

149
00:13:05,209 --> 00:13:09,165
You saved my life!
God, that's embarrassing!

150
00:13:09,205 --> 00:13:12,111
Telling me!

151
00:13:12,202 --> 00:13:15,679
He wanted me dead.

152
00:13:15,719 --> 00:13:18,797
And he's still out there, Mickey.

153
00:13:18,837 --> 00:13:22,972
That policeman. . .that thing!

154
00:13:27,588 --> 00:13:29,625
Right, you head off.

155
00:13:29,665 --> 00:13:33,261
Inform control I've one or two
things that still need doing.

156
00:13:33,301 --> 00:13:36,662
I haven't quite finished
with Mrs Tyler yet.

157
00:13:42,809 --> 00:13:45,860
Is that all of us?

158
00:13:45,126 --> 00:13:50,251
All the family except Sip Fel Fotch.
He's found a hunt of his own. Ah.

159
00:13:53,799 --> 00:13:55,754
What was his name?

160
00:13:55,794 --> 00:14:00,593
Who? This one. The, er, secretary,
or whatever he was called.

161
00:14:00,633 --> 00:14:03,549
I don't know. I talked to him.

162
00:14:03,589 --> 00:14:05,987
I brought him a cup of coffee.

163
00:14:06,027 --> 00:14:07,705
I never asked his name.

164
00:14:10,856 --> 00:14:12,300
Sorry.

165
00:14:14,534 --> 00:14:17,492
Right, what have we got?
Any terminals, anything?

166
00:14:17,532 --> 00:14:19,648
Nope, this place is antique.

167
00:14:19,688 --> 00:14:25,600
When they killed the Prime Minister,
why didn't they use him as
a disguise? He's too slim.

168
00:14:25,046 --> 00:14:27,363
They need to fit inside big humans.

169
00:14:27,403 --> 00:14:30,756
But the Slitheen are eight feet.
How do they squeeze inside?

170
00:14:30,796 --> 00:14:35,112
That's the device round their
necks, it shrinks them down a bit.

171
00:14:35,152 --> 00:14:37,511
That's why there's all that gas.

172
00:14:37,551 --> 00:14:40,187
Wish I had a compression field.

173
00:14:40,227 --> 00:14:43,943
Excuse me, people are dead!
This is not the time for jokes.

174
00:14:43,983 --> 00:14:46,942
You get used to this
stuff when you're friends with him.

175
00:14:46,982 --> 00:14:48,856
Well, that's a strange friendship.

176
00:14:48,896 --> 00:14:51,176
Harriet Jones. I've heard that name
before.

177
00:14:51,216 --> 00:14:53,653
You're not famous for anything,
are you?

178
00:14:53,693 --> 00:14:57,129
Hardly! Rings a bell. Harriet Jones?

179
00:14:57,169 --> 00:15:00,600
Lifelong backbencher and a fat lot
of use of I am now.

180
00:15:00,046 --> 00:15:01,723
The Protocols are redundant.

181
00:15:01,763 --> 00:15:04,481
They list people who could help
and they're dead.

182
00:15:04,521 --> 00:15:08,758
Couldn't we just
launch a nuclear bomb at them?

183
00:15:08,798 --> 00:15:10,915
You're a very violent young woman.

184
00:15:10,955 --> 00:15:14,320
I'm serious, we could!
There's nothing like that in here.

185
00:15:14,072 --> 00:15:19,150
Nuclear strikes do need a release
code, yes, but it's kept secret
by the United Nations.

186
00:15:19,190 --> 00:15:21,466
Say that again.
What, about the codes?

187
00:15:21,506 --> 00:15:23,660
Anything, all of it.

188
00:15:23,700 --> 00:15:29,536
The British Isles can't gain access
to atomic weapons without
a Special Resolution from the UN.

189
00:15:29,576 --> 00:15:35,610
Like that's ever stopped 'em.
Exactly, given our past record. . .
and I voted against that.

190
00:15:35,650 --> 00:15:39,682
The codes have been given to the UN.
Is it important?

191
00:15:39,722 --> 00:15:43,323
Everything's important. If only
we knew what the Slitheen wanted.

192
00:15:43,363 --> 00:15:47,595
I'm saying Slitheen as if it's
normal! What do they want, though?

193
00:15:47,635 --> 00:15:51,551
They're just one family,
so it's not an invasion.

194
00:15:51,591 --> 00:15:56,260
They're out to make money.
They want to use something on Earth.

195
00:15:56,066 --> 00:15:58,424
Some kind of asset. Like gold?

196
00:15:58,464 --> 00:16:00,385
Oil? Water?

197
00:16:00,425 --> 00:16:02,978
You're very good at this.

198
00:16:03,018 --> 00:16:06,373
Thank you. Harriet Jones,
why do I know that name?

199
00:16:06,413 --> 00:16:09,773
PHONE BEEPS
Oh, that's me.
How did you get a signal?

200
00:16:09,813 --> 00:16:12,210
He zapped it. Super phone.

201
00:16:12,250 --> 00:16:14,246
Then we can phone your contacts!

202
00:16:14,286 --> 00:16:15,964
Dead downstairs, yeah.

203
00:16:16,004 --> 00:16:17,845
It's Mickey.

204
00:16:17,885 --> 00:16:20,280
Oh, tell your stupid
boyfriend we're busy.

205
00:16:20,320 --> 00:16:22,293
Yeah, he's not so stupid after all.

206
00:16:27,274 --> 00:16:29,872
No, no, not just alien,

207
00:16:29,912 --> 00:16:33,906
but like, proper alien, all stinking
and wet and disgusting,

208
00:16:33,946 --> 00:16:37,107
and more to the point,
it wanted to kill us!

209
00:16:37,147 --> 00:16:39,740
I could have died!
Is she all right though?

210
00:16:39,780 --> 00:16:41,300
Don't put her on, just tell me.

211
00:16:41,340 --> 00:16:44,950
Is that Ricky?
Just go to your computer.

212
00:16:44,135 --> 00:16:45,651
It's Mickey, and why should I?

213
00:16:45,691 --> 00:16:50,532
Mickey, the idiot,
I might just choke before I finish
this sentence, but. . .I need you.

214
00:16:52,245 --> 00:16:54,325
It says "password?"

215
00:16:54,365 --> 00:16:55,962
Say again.

216
00:16:56,002 --> 00:16:57,521
It's asking for the password.

217
00:16:57,561 --> 00:16:59,719
Buffalo. Two Fs, one L.

218
00:16:59,759 --> 00:17:01,562
So what's that website?

219
00:17:03,075 --> 00:17:05,913
All the secret
information known to mankind.

220
00:17:05,953 --> 00:17:09,747
See, they've known about aliens for
years. They just kept us in the dark.

221
00:17:09,787 --> 00:17:11,468
Mickey, you were born in the dark.

222
00:17:11,508 --> 00:17:13,308
Oh, leave him alone.
Thank you.

223
00:17:13,348 --> 00:17:15,924
Password again.
Just repeat it, every time.

224
00:17:19,456 --> 00:17:22,373
Big Ben. Why did the Slitheen go
and hit Big Ben?

225
00:17:22,413 --> 00:17:24,693
You said to gather the experts,
to kill them.

226
00:17:24,733 --> 00:17:28,210
That lot would've gathered for a
weather balloon. You don't need to
crash-land in the middle of London.

227
00:17:28,250 --> 00:17:32,363
The Slitheen are hiding,
but then they put the entire
planet on red alert. Why?

228
00:17:32,403 --> 00:17:35,961
Oh, listen to her(!)
Oi! At least I'm trying.

229
00:17:36,001 --> 00:17:37,997
I'VE got a question,
if you don't mind.

230
00:17:38,037 --> 00:17:42,235
Since that man walked
into our lives,
I've been attacked in the streets,

231
00:17:42,275 --> 00:17:45,869
I've had creatures from the pits
of hell in my own living room

232
00:17:45,909 --> 00:17:50,785
and my daughter disappear off the
face of the Earth. I told you
what happened. I'm talking to HIM!

233
00:17:50,825 --> 00:17:54,497
Cos I've seen this life of yours,
Doctor, and maybe you get off on it,

234
00:17:54,537 --> 00:17:59,933
and maybe you think it's all
clever and smart, but you tell me -
just answer me this -

235
00:17:59,973 --> 00:18:02,650
is my daughter safe?

236
00:18:02,690 --> 00:18:04,648
I'm fine.

237
00:18:04,688 --> 00:18:06,685
Is she safe? !

238
00:18:06,725 --> 00:18:11,315
Will she always be safe,
can you promise me that?

239
00:18:15,399 --> 00:18:17,776
Well, what's the answer?

240
00:18:21,393 --> 00:18:23,270
We're in.

241
00:18:23,310 --> 00:18:25,187
Now, then. On the left, at the top,

242
00:18:25,227 --> 00:18:29,317
there's a tab, an icon, little
concentric circles, click on that.

243
00:18:32,218 --> 00:18:34,535
What is it? The Slitheen have got
a spaceship in the North Sea

244
00:18:34,575 --> 00:18:37,812
transmitting that signal. Now, hush,
let me work out what it's saying.

245
00:18:37,852 --> 00:18:41,251
He'll have to answer me one day.
Hush! It's some sort of message.

246
00:18:41,291 --> 00:18:44,328
What's it say? Don't know,
it's on a loop, keeps repeating.

247
00:18:44,368 --> 00:18:46,844
BUZZ!
Hush! That's not me!

248
00:18:46,884 --> 00:18:52,942
Go and see who that is.
It's three o'clock in the morning.
Well, go and tell THEM that!

249
00:18:53,036 --> 00:18:55,948
It's beaming out into space.
Who's it for?

250
00:18:57,871 --> 00:19:00,453
DOORBELL BUZZES
All right!

251
00:19:01,708 --> 00:19:03,517
Mrs Tyler.

252
00:19:04,186 --> 00:19:05,787
Ooh!

253
00:19:05,827 --> 00:19:10,857
It's him, it's the thing! It's
the Slickeen! They've found us!

254
00:19:10,897 --> 00:19:15,614
Mickey, I need that signal.
Never mind the signal, get out!
Mum just get out!

255
00:19:15,654 --> 00:19:18,151
We can't, it's by the front door.

256
00:19:27,842 --> 00:19:29,959
Oh, my God.

257
00:19:29,999 --> 00:19:32,996
It's unmasking. It's gonna kill us.

258
00:19:33,036 --> 00:19:37,710
There's got to be some way of
stopping them! You're supposed to be
the expert - think of something!

259
00:19:37,750 --> 00:19:39,310
I'm trying!

260
00:19:39,350 --> 00:19:43,184
RUMBLES AND SQUELCHES
I'll take it on, Jackie.
You just run.

261
00:19:43,224 --> 00:19:46,221
Don't look back. Just run.

262
00:19:46,261 --> 00:19:49,338
CLATTERS AND THUDS
That's my mother.

263
00:19:50,815 --> 00:19:54,332
Right! If we're gonna find
their weakness, we need to find out
where they're from, which planet.

264
00:19:54,372 --> 00:19:58,700
Their basic shape, that narrows it
down to 5, 000 nearby planets.

265
00:19:58,047 --> 00:20:02,562
What else do we know about them?
Information! They're green! Narrows
it down. Good sense of smell.

266
00:20:02,602 --> 00:20:06,598
Narrows it down. They can smell
adrenaline. Narrows it down.
Pig technology. Narrows it down.

267
00:20:06,638 --> 00:20:09,795
Spaceship in the Thames.
Slipstream engine. Narrows it down.

268
00:20:10,518 --> 00:20:12,201
It's getting in!

269
00:20:14,231 --> 00:20:18,469
They hunt, like a ritual. Narrows
it down. Wait, did you notice. . . ?

270
00:20:18,509 --> 00:20:23,699
When they fart, if you'll pardon
the word, it doesn't just smell like
a fart, if you'll pardon the word,

271
00:20:23,739 --> 00:20:29,934
it's something else? What? It's more
like, um. . . Bad breath! That's it!
Calcium decay! THAT narrows it down!

272
00:20:29,974 --> 00:20:31,743
We're getting there, Mum! Too late!

273
00:20:34,248 --> 00:20:39,364
Calcium phosphate, organic calcium,
living calcium, creatures made
out of living calcium. What else?

274
00:20:39,404 --> 00:20:42,321
Hyphenated surname! YES!
That narrows it down to one planet!

275
00:20:42,361 --> 00:20:44,439
Raxacoricofallapatorius!

276
00:20:44,479 --> 00:20:46,248
Great, we can write 'em a letter(!)

277
00:20:48,677 --> 00:20:50,929
Get into the kitchen!

278
00:20:56,747 --> 00:20:59,501
Oh, God, it's going to rip us apart!

279
00:20:59,541 --> 00:21:03,298
Calcium weakened by the compression
field. . . Acetic acid! Vinegar!

280
00:21:03,338 --> 00:21:06,853
Just like Hannibal! Just like
Hannibal! Mickey, have you got
any vinegar? How should I know?!

281
00:21:06,893 --> 00:21:09,920
It's your kitchen!
Cupboard by the sink, middle shelf!

282
00:21:09,132 --> 00:21:12,409
Oh, give it here. What d'you need?
Anything with vinegar.

283
00:21:12,449 --> 00:21:14,486
Gherkins!

284
00:21:14,526 --> 00:21:16,326
SLITHEEN ROARS

285
00:21:16,366 --> 00:21:18,874
Yeah, pickled onion!

286
00:21:20,124 --> 00:21:22,991
Pickled eggs!
 .You KISS this man?

287
00:21:24,954 --> 00:21:27,154
IT SCREECHES

288
00:21:27,194 --> 00:21:29,327
IT HISSES

289
00:21:33,229 --> 00:21:34,901
PHRRRRRT!

290
00:21:49,128 --> 00:21:50,807
Hannibal?

291
00:21:50,847 --> 00:21:56,600
Hannibal crossed the Alps
by dissolving boulders with vinegar.

292
00:21:57,122 --> 00:21:59,135
Oh. Well, there you go, then.

293
00:22:08,349 --> 00:22:12,665
He's dead. Sip Fel Fotch
Pasameer-Day Slitheen is dead.

294
00:22:12,705 --> 00:22:14,145
I felt it.

295
00:22:14,185 --> 00:22:15,738
How could that happen?

296
00:22:15,778 --> 00:22:17,859
Somebody must've got lucky.

297
00:22:17,899 --> 00:22:22,728
That's the last piece of luck
anyone on this rock will ever have.

298
00:22:32,322 --> 00:22:34,320
Ladies and gentlemen. . .

299
00:22:34,360 --> 00:22:36,880
nations of the world.

300
00:22:36,920 --> 00:22:38,676
Humankind.

301
00:22:38,716 --> 00:22:44,320
The greatest experts
in extraterrestrial events
came here tonight.

302
00:22:44,072 --> 00:22:46,588
They gathered in the common cause.

303
00:22:46,628 --> 00:22:52,222
But the news I bring you now is
grave indeed.

304
00:22:52,262 --> 00:22:55,815
The experts are dead.

305
00:22:55,855 --> 00:23:00,534
Murdered, right in front of me,
by alien hands.

306
00:23:00,574 --> 00:23:03,110
Peoples of the Earth, heed my words.

307
00:23:03,051 --> 00:23:07,450
These "visitors"
do not come in peace.

308
00:23:07,085 --> 00:23:08,884
Listen to this!

309
00:23:08,924 --> 00:23:14,279
'Our inspectors have searched
the sky above our heads.'

310
00:23:14,319 --> 00:23:16,116
And they have found. . .

311
00:23:16,156 --> 00:23:19,116
MASSIVE weapons of destruction

312
00:23:19,156 --> 00:23:24,290
'capable of being deployed
within 45 seconds. . .' What?

313
00:23:24,069 --> 00:23:29,981
Our technicians can baffle
the alien probes,

314
00:23:30,021 --> 00:23:32,970
but not for long.

315
00:23:32,137 --> 00:23:35,135
'We are facing extinction.

316
00:23:35,175 --> 00:23:38,470
'Unless we strike first!'

317
00:23:39,809 --> 00:23:45,325
'The United Kingdom stands directly
beneath the belly of the mothership.'

318
00:23:45,365 --> 00:23:48,404
I beg of the United Nations.

319
00:23:48,444 --> 00:23:52,917
Pass an emergency resolution.
Give us the access codes.

320
00:23:52,957 --> 00:23:58,670
A nuclear strike at the heart of the
beast is our only chance of survival.

321
00:23:58,710 --> 00:24:00,679
Because. . .

322
00:24:00,719 --> 00:24:03,250
from this moment on,

323
00:24:03,065 --> 00:24:07,166
it is my solemn duty to inform you. . .

324
00:24:09,059 --> 00:24:11,935
planet Earth is at war.

325
00:24:11,975 --> 00:24:17,500
He's making it up! There's no weapon
up there, no threat. He invented it!
Do you think they'll believe him?

326
00:24:17,090 --> 00:24:21,567
You did last time. That's why
the Slitheen went for spectacle.
They want the whole world panicking

327
00:24:21,607 --> 00:24:26,800
cos you lot, you get scared, you
lash out. . . Release the defence
codes. The Slitheen go nuclear. Why?

328
00:24:26,840 --> 00:24:31,765
You get the codes, release
the missiles. But not into space -

329
00:24:31,805 --> 00:24:36,354
there's nothing there. You attack
every other country on Earth.
They fight back.

330
00:24:36,394 --> 00:24:39,584
World War Three.
Whole planet gets nuked.

331
00:24:39,624 --> 00:24:42,240
And we sit through it, safe in our
spaceship, waiting in the Thames.

332
00:24:42,064 --> 00:24:46,858
Not crashed, just parked.
Barely two minutes away.

333
00:24:46,898 --> 00:24:50,495
But you'll destroy the planet.
This beautiful place. What for?

334
00:24:50,535 --> 00:24:54,418
Profit. That's what the signal is,
beaming into space. An advert.

335
00:24:54,458 --> 00:24:59,445
Sale of the century! We reduce the
Earth to molten slag, then sell it.

336
00:24:59,485 --> 00:25:01,972
Piece by piece.

337
00:25:02,012 --> 00:25:03,840
Radioactive chunks

338
00:25:03,880 --> 00:25:07,916
capable of powering every cut-price
starliner and budget cargo ship.

339
00:25:07,956 --> 00:25:11,674
There's a recession out there,
Doctor. People are buying cheap.

340
00:25:11,714 --> 00:25:14,271
This rock becomes raw fuel.

341
00:25:14,311 --> 00:25:16,108
At the cost of five billion lives.

342
00:25:16,148 --> 00:25:17,945
Huh! Bargain.

343
00:25:17,985 --> 00:25:21,472
I give you a choice. Leave this
planet, or I'll stop you.

344
00:25:24,746 --> 00:25:26,907
What? You?

345
00:25:27,856 --> 00:25:29,824
Trapped in your box?

346
00:25:31,093 --> 00:25:32,936
Yes. Me.

347
00:25:55,073 --> 00:25:59,419
Yesterday saw the start
of a brave new world.

348
00:25:59,459 --> 00:26:01,523
Today might see it end.

349
00:26:02,507 --> 00:26:04,814
The streets are deserted.

350
00:26:04,854 --> 00:26:07,620
Everyone's home, just waiting,

351
00:26:07,660 --> 00:26:11,340
as the future is decided
in New York.

352
00:26:14,566 --> 00:26:17,442
AMERICAN REPORTER: It's midnight
here in New York.

353
00:26:17,482 --> 00:26:19,368
The United Nations has gathered.

354
00:26:19,408 --> 00:26:25,593
England has provided them with
absolute proof that the massive
weapons of destruction do exist.

355
00:26:25,633 --> 00:26:28,872
'The Security Council will make the
resolution in a matter of minutes.

356
00:26:28,912 --> 00:26:31,633
'And once the codes are released,

357
00:26:31,673 --> 00:26:36,130
'humanity's first
interplanetary war begins.'

358
00:26:45,973 --> 00:26:49,250
Sergeant!

359
00:26:49,290 --> 00:26:51,968
We'll take the call
in the Prime Minister's office.

360
00:26:52,008 --> 00:26:54,765
Maintain your positions.

361
00:26:54,805 --> 00:26:56,983
Good luck.

362
00:27:03,244 --> 00:27:05,272
EXCITED BUSTLE

363
00:27:05,312 --> 00:27:09,300
Look at that!
The telephone is actually red!

364
00:27:10,186 --> 00:27:11,704
PHRRRT!

365
00:27:11,744 --> 00:27:14,660
How long till they phone?

366
00:27:14,700 --> 00:27:16,958
Counting down. . .

367
00:27:18,822 --> 00:27:23,510
OK, Doctor.
I'm not saying I trust you, but
there must be something you can do.

368
00:27:23,091 --> 00:27:26,970
If we could ferment the port,
we could make acetic acid.

369
00:27:26,137 --> 00:27:28,966
Mickey, any luck?
There's loads of emergency numbers.

370
00:27:29,006 --> 00:27:32,803
They're all on voicemail.
Voicemail dooms us all.

371
00:27:32,843 --> 00:27:38,850
If we could just get out of here.
There's a way out. What?
There's always been a way out.

372
00:27:38,125 --> 00:27:40,874
Then why don't we use it?

373
00:27:40,914 --> 00:27:46,664
Because I can't guarantee your
daughter will be safe. Don't dare!
Whatever it is, don't you dare!

374
00:27:46,704 --> 00:27:51,465
That's the thing. If I don't dare,
everyone dies. Do it.

375
00:27:52,938 --> 00:27:57,693
You don't even know what it is.
You'd just let me? Yeah.

376
00:27:59,296 --> 00:28:03,770
Please, Doctor, please!
She's my daughter, she's just a kid.

377
00:28:03,810 --> 00:28:09,830
Do you think I don't know that?
Cos this is my life, Jackie.
It's not fun, it's not smart,

378
00:28:09,123 --> 00:28:13,964
it's standing up and making a
decision. Because nobody else will.
Then what are you waiting for?

379
00:28:15,046 --> 00:28:17,332
I could save the world,
but lose you.

380
00:28:26,383 --> 00:28:31,140
Except it's not your decision,
Doctor. It's mine.

381
00:28:31,180 --> 00:28:35,494
And who the hell are you? Harriet
Jones, MP for Flydale North.

382
00:28:35,534 --> 00:28:39,968
The only elected representative
in this room. Chosen by the people
for the people,

383
00:28:40,008 --> 00:28:43,859
and on behalf of the people,
I command you. Do it.

384
00:28:47,283 --> 00:28:49,167
Victory. . .

385
00:28:49,207 --> 00:28:51,909
should be naked!

386
00:29:01,312 --> 00:29:04,907
How do we get out?
We don't. We stay here.

387
00:29:08,579 --> 00:29:12,845
The Council is voting. The result
should be known any second now.

388
00:29:20,566 --> 00:29:23,230
Use the buffalo password,
it overrides everything.

389
00:29:24,084 --> 00:29:26,640
What are you doing?

390
00:29:26,680 --> 00:29:28,653
Hacking into the Royal Navy.

391
00:29:29,438 --> 00:29:33,315
We're in! Here it is, HMS Taurean,
Trafalgar Class submarine,

392
00:29:33,355 --> 00:29:37,393
ten miles off the coast of Plymouth.
Right, we need to select a missile.

393
00:29:37,433 --> 00:29:39,586
We can't go nuclear.
We don't have the defence codes.

394
00:29:39,626 --> 00:29:43,539
We don't need it, just an ordinary
missile. What's the first category?

395
00:29:43,579 --> 00:29:47,617
Sub harpoon, UGM-84A.
That's the one! Select!

396
00:29:47,657 --> 00:29:50,199
I could stop you.

397
00:29:52,891 --> 00:29:54,499
Do it then.

398
00:29:54,539 --> 00:29:56,308
Ready for this?

399
00:29:58,285 --> 00:29:59,400
Yeah.

400
00:30:04,002 --> 00:30:07,716
Mickey the idiot,
the world is in your hands.

401
00:30:07,756 --> 00:30:09,399
Fire!

402
00:30:18,545 --> 00:30:20,593
(Oh, my God.)

403
00:30:26,942 --> 00:30:29,604
STEADY BLIPS

404
00:30:31,649 --> 00:30:33,767
How solid are these?

405
00:30:33,807 --> 00:30:38,803
Not solid enough.
Built for short-range attack,
nothing this big. All right. . .

406
00:30:38,843 --> 00:30:43,244
I'M making a decision. I'm not gonna
die. We're gonna ride this one out.

407
00:30:43,284 --> 00:30:47,354
With earthquakes, you can survive
by standing under a doorframe.

408
00:30:47,394 --> 00:30:51,165
This cupboard's small, so it's
strong. Come and help. Come on!

409
00:30:56,589 --> 00:30:58,819
The vote is in.

410
00:30:58,859 --> 00:31:01,830
The Council says. . .

411
00:31:02,183 --> 00:31:03,303
yes.

412
00:31:03,343 --> 00:31:06,170
They are releasing the codes.

413
00:31:07,174 --> 00:31:09,211
Ring, damn you!

414
00:31:09,251 --> 00:31:11,288
RAPID BLIPS
It's on radar!

415
00:31:11,328 --> 00:31:13,211
Counter defence 556.

416
00:31:13,251 --> 00:31:17,560
Stop them intercepting it!
I'm doing it now! Good boy.

417
00:31:19,406 --> 00:31:21,249
556 neutralised.

418
00:31:27,400 --> 00:31:29,937
What do you mean, "incoming"?

419
00:31:39,578 --> 00:31:43,924
ALARM SHRIEKS
Everybody out! Now! Now!

420
00:31:44,732 --> 00:31:47,569
What the hell is that for?

421
00:31:47,609 --> 00:31:50,646
Sir, there's a missile. . .

422
00:31:50,686 --> 00:31:52,170
Sorry!

423
00:32:07,629 --> 00:32:09,309
That's mine. You've got mine!

424
00:32:09,349 --> 00:32:11,383
Disguise me, disguise me!

425
00:32:11,423 --> 00:32:16,218
No, you're the blonde.
I want the other one, take it off!

426
00:32:18,137 --> 00:32:20,894
GUNSHOT
Everybody, run!

427
00:32:20,934 --> 00:32:23,451
Run! Let's get out of here!

428
00:32:23,491 --> 00:32:27,570
MISSILE SCREECHES

429
00:32:30,245 --> 00:32:32,820
Here we go.

430
00:32:32,122 --> 00:32:34,425
Well, nice knowing you both.

431
00:32:35,400 --> 00:32:36,469
Hannibal!

432
00:32:37,683 --> 00:32:39,241
Oh, bol. . .

433
00:33:12,843 --> 00:33:14,677
Made in Britain!

434
00:33:14,717 --> 00:33:17,740
Oh, my God. Are you all right?

435
00:33:17,114 --> 00:33:21,348
Harriet Jones, MP, Flydale North.
Contact the UN immediately.

436
00:33:21,388 --> 00:33:26,382
Tell the ambassadors the crisis
is over, they can step down.
Go on, tell the news!

437
00:33:26,422 --> 00:33:30,380
Yes, ma'am. Someone's got a hell
of a job sorting this lot out.

438
00:33:30,420 --> 00:33:33,417
Oh, Lord! We haven't even got
a Prime Minister!

439
00:33:33,457 --> 00:33:36,895
Maybe you should have a go.
Me! I'm only a backbencher.

440
00:33:36,935 --> 00:33:39,967
I'd vote for ya.
Now, don't be silly.

441
00:33:40,971 --> 00:33:43,209
I'd better go and see if I can help.

442
00:33:43,249 --> 00:33:45,996
EMERGENCY SIRENS APPROACH
Hang on!

443
00:33:48,203 --> 00:33:51,809
We're safe - the Earth is safe!

444
00:33:52,796 --> 00:33:55,275
Sergeant!

445
00:33:55,315 --> 00:33:57,510
I thought I knew the name.

446
00:33:57,550 --> 00:33:59,787
Harriet Jones.

447
00:33:59,827 --> 00:34:06,261
Future Prime Minister, elected
for three successive terms. The
architect of Britain's Golden Age.

448
00:34:06,301 --> 00:34:09,615
THE CRISIS HAS PASSED!

449
00:34:09,655 --> 00:34:13,267
Ladies and gentlemen, I have
something to say to you all. . .

450
00:34:14,573 --> 00:34:16,929
Here today. . .

451
00:34:16,969 --> 00:34:20,727
 .mankind stands tall -

452
00:34:20,767 --> 00:34:23,403
proud and undefeated!

453
00:34:23,443 --> 00:34:26,486
God bless the human race.

454
00:34:51,854 --> 00:34:54,731
TARDIS CLANKS AND WHEEZES

455
00:34:54,771 --> 00:34:58,804
HARRIET ON TV:
'Mankind stands tall. . .'

456
00:34:58,844 --> 00:35:01,966
Harriet Jones -
who does she think she is?

457
00:35:02,006 --> 00:35:05,999
Look at her, taking all the credit.
It should be you on there.

458
00:35:06,039 --> 00:35:09,910
MY DAUGHTER saved the world!
I think the Doctor helped a bit(!)

459
00:35:09,950 --> 00:35:12,950
All right, then, him too.

460
00:35:12,990 --> 00:35:17,630
You should be given knighthoods.
It's not the way he does things.

461
00:35:17,103 --> 00:35:21,782
No fuss, he just moves on. He's not
that bad, if you gave him a chance.

462
00:35:21,822 --> 00:35:27,150
He's good in a crisis, I'll give him
that. Oh-h-h! The world HAS changed.

463
00:35:27,055 --> 00:35:32,332
You're saying nice things about him.
I've no choice. There's no getting
rid of him, since you're infatuated.

464
00:35:32,372 --> 00:35:34,860
I'm not infatuated!

465
00:35:34,126 --> 00:35:36,887
What does he eat? How d'you mean?

466
00:35:36,927 --> 00:35:41,999
I was gonna do shepherd's pie.
All of us, proper sit-down.

467
00:35:42,039 --> 00:35:43,637
Cos. . .

468
00:35:43,677 --> 00:35:48,831
I'm ready to listen.
I wanna learn about you and 'im
and that life you lead.

469
00:35:48,871 --> 00:35:54,585
Only, I dunno, he's an alien.
For all I know he eats
grass and safety pins and things.

470
00:35:54,625 --> 00:35:59,699
He'll have shepherds pie. . .
You're gonna cook for him?
What's wrong with that?

471
00:35:59,739 --> 00:36:02,176
He's finally met his match.

472
00:36:02,216 --> 00:36:05,214
You're not too old for a slap,
you know!

473
00:36:05,254 --> 00:36:07,933
We can go and visit your gran
tomorrow.

474
00:36:07,973 --> 00:36:11,729
PHONE RINGS
Better learn some French.
I told her you were in France.

475
00:36:11,769 --> 00:36:14,562
I said you were au-pairing. . .

476
00:36:15,805 --> 00:36:17,325
Hello?

477
00:36:17,365 --> 00:36:21,278
Right, I'll be a couple of hours,
then we can go. You've got a phone!

478
00:36:21,318 --> 00:36:26,233
You think I can travel through space
and time and I haven't got a phone?
Like I said, couple of hours.

479
00:36:26,273 --> 00:36:28,668
I've just got to send a dispersal. . .

480
00:36:28,708 --> 00:36:34,640
There, that's cancelling
out the Slitheens' advert in case
any bargains hunters turn up.

481
00:36:34,104 --> 00:36:36,261
Um, my mother's cooking.

482
00:36:36,301 --> 00:36:41,455
Good, put her on a slow heat
and let her simmer. She's cooking
tea. For us. I don't do that.

483
00:36:41,495 --> 00:36:47,209
She wants to get to know you.
Tough, I've better things to do.
It's just tea. Not to me, it isn't.

484
00:36:47,249 --> 00:36:54,163
She's my mother. Well, she's
not MINE. That's not fair. Well,
you can stay there if you want.

485
00:36:54,203 --> 00:36:57,517
But here's this plasma storm
brewing in the Horsehead Nebula.

486
00:36:57,557 --> 00:37:00,394
Fires are burning,
ten million miles wide.

487
00:37:00,434 --> 00:37:05,188
I could fly the Tardis
into the heart of it then ride
the shockwave all the way out,

488
00:37:05,228 --> 00:37:08,840
hurtle right across the sky
and end up anywhere.

489
00:37:10,622 --> 00:37:12,271
Your choice.

490
00:37:24,771 --> 00:37:27,168
Rose? I was thinking,

491
00:37:27,208 --> 00:37:32,947
I've got that bottle of Amaretto
from New Year's Eve. Does he drink?

492
00:37:37,114 --> 00:37:40,550
I was wondering whether he drinks
or not. Yeah, he does.

493
00:37:45,907 --> 00:37:48,104
Don't go, sweetheart.

494
00:37:48,144 --> 00:37:51,160
Please don't go.

495
00:38:06,605 --> 00:38:10,985
Good lad. Graffiti that again
and I'll 'ave ya. Now, beat it.

496
00:38:15,915 --> 00:38:18,471
I just went down the shop.

497
00:38:18,511 --> 00:38:23,105
And I was thinking, y'know,
like, the whole world's changed.

498
00:38:23,145 --> 00:38:25,744
Aliens and spaceships all in public.

499
00:38:25,784 --> 00:38:28,420
And here it is.

500
00:38:28,460 --> 00:38:31,857
How can they do that? They SAW it!
You're just not ready.

501
00:38:31,897 --> 00:38:36,334
You're happy to believe in something
that's invisible, but if it's
staring you in the face - "Nope,

502
00:38:36,374 --> 00:38:40,509
"can't see it!" There's a scientific
explanation for that - you're thick.

503
00:38:42,607 --> 00:38:46,562
We're just idiots.
Well, not all of you. Yeah?

504
00:38:46,602 --> 00:38:48,680
Present for you, Mickey.

505
00:38:48,720 --> 00:38:53,752
That's a virus. Put it online,
it'll destroy every mention of me.
I'll cease to exist.

506
00:38:53,792 --> 00:38:58,746
What d'you wanna do that for?
Cos you're right. I AM dangerous.
I don't want anybody following me.

507
00:38:59,666 --> 00:39:03,640
How can you say that,
and then take her with you?

508
00:39:03,104 --> 00:39:06,180
You could look after her.
Come with us.

509
00:39:06,220 --> 00:39:08,297
I can't.

510
00:39:08,337 --> 00:39:10,575
This life of yours. . .

511
00:39:10,615 --> 00:39:13,203
it's just too much, I couldn't do it.

512
00:39:14,132 --> 00:39:16,329
Don't tell her I said that.

513
00:39:16,369 --> 00:39:19,885
I'll get a proper job. I'll work
weekends, I'll pass my test.

514
00:39:19,925 --> 00:39:24,430
If Jim comes round again,
I'll say no, I really will.
I'm not leaving cos of you.

515
00:39:24,083 --> 00:39:27,874
I'm travelling, that's all, and then
I'll come back. But it's not safe.

516
00:39:27,914 --> 00:39:32,140
Mum. If you saw it out there,
you'd never stay home.

517
00:39:34,508 --> 00:39:39,250
Got enough stuff(? )
The first time I stepped in there,
it was spur-of-the-moment.

518
00:39:39,065 --> 00:39:42,142
Now I'm signing up.
You're stuck with me.

519
00:39:44,579 --> 00:39:46,976
Come with us.
There's plenty of room.

520
00:39:47,016 --> 00:39:50,253
No chance. He's a liability,
I'm not having him on board.

521
00:39:50,293 --> 00:39:53,495
We'd be dead without him. My
decision is final.

522
00:39:55,447 --> 00:39:56,800
Sorry.

523
00:39:59,444 --> 00:40:01,562
Good luck, yeah?

524
00:40:01,602 --> 00:40:06,275
You still can't promise me. What if
she gets lost? What if something
happens to YOU, Doctor,

525
00:40:06,315 --> 00:40:11,830
and she's left all alone, standing
on some moon a million light years
away? How long do I wait then?

526
00:40:11,870 --> 00:40:15,544
Mum, you're forgetting -
it's a time machine.

527
00:40:15,584 --> 00:40:20,420
I could travel round suns
and planets all the way out
to the edge of the universe,

528
00:40:20,460 --> 00:40:25,535
and by the time I get back, yeah,
ten seconds would've passed.
Just ten seconds.

529
00:40:25,575 --> 00:40:28,120
So stop worrying.

530
00:40:28,052 --> 00:40:31,618
I'll see you in ten seconds' time,
yeah?

531
00:41:12,967 --> 00:41:14,605
Ten seconds.

532
00:41:35,581 --> 00:41:39,415
 .It's a great big museum. An alien
museum. Someone's got a hobby.

533
00:41:39,455 --> 00:41:44,291
The cage contains my one living
specimen. . . . .It must've fallen
through time, the only survivor.

534
00:41:44,331 --> 00:41:45,729
It's killing him. Do something!

535
00:41:45,769 --> 00:41:49,845
I swear no-one on this base is safe.
 .You've gotta keep it in that cell.

536
00:41:49,885 --> 00:41:53,566
 .It can't get out. That lock's
got a billion combinations. . .

537
00:41:53,606 --> 00:41:55,460
 .Civilians - let 'em through!

538
00:41:55,086 --> 00:41:57,837
 .That thing is gonna kill
every one of us!

539
00:41:57,877 --> 00:42:01,354
The nearest town? Salt Lake City.
Population? One million. All dead.

540
00:42:01,394 --> 00:42:03,191
If you want orders, follow this one.

541
00:42:03,231 --> 00:42:04,628
 .Why don't you just DIE? !

542
00:42:04,668 --> 00:42:06,268
 .We're nearly there! Two seconds.

543
00:42:06,308 --> 00:42:08,625
I've come to help. I'm The Doctor.

544
00:42:08,665 --> 00:42:09,985
DALEK: Exterminate!

545
00:42:10,025 --> 00:42:12,780
Impossible.
DALEK: EXTERMINATE!

546
00:42:45,333 --> 00:42:49,429
Subtitles by BBC Broadcast - 2005
0
